.\" trash-list(1) manual page for trash-cli.
.TH TRASH-LIST 1 2026-11-14 "trash-cli 0.26.9.29" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trash\-list \- list trashed files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trash\-list
[\fB\-h\fR]
[\fB\-\-print\-completion {\fIbash\fB,\fIzsh\fB,\fItcsh\fB}\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-\-volumes\fR]
[\fB\-\-trash\-dirs\fR]
[\fB\-\-trash\-dir \fITRASH_DIRS\fR]
[\fB\-\-all\-users\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trash\-list
prints one line per trashed file:
.RS
.PP
.nf
<YYYY\-MM\-DD HH:MM:SS> <original location>
.fi
.RE
.PP
The date is the
.B DeletionDate
of the trashinfo file, local time; a missing or invalid date renders as the
literal
\fB????\-??\-?? ??:??:??\fR.
The original location is reconstructed from the
.B Path=
field of the trashinfo file.
Entries are collected from every
\fI<trash\-dir>/info/*.trashinfo\fR
of every trash can scanned, trash cans in scan order (home trash first, then
volumes) and entries in directory order: there is no sorting and no
deduplication.
Orphans, entries in
.I files/
without an info file, are never listed.
.PP
When standard output is a terminal, paths are shell\-quoted GNU\-ls\-style so
that they cannot inject terminal escapes, unless
.B TRASH_RAW_OUTPUT
is set.
Output through a pipe is raw, with bytes that are not valid UTF\-8 rendered
as
\fB\\xNN\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-completion {\fIbash\fR,\fIzsh\fR,\fItcsh\fR}
Parse the option and exit 0; this implementation prints
Please install shtab firstly! (upstream required the optional shtab
module to emit a script).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the program name and version, for example
\fBtrash\-list 0.26.9.29\fR,
and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-volumes
Print the volume list, one path per line, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-trash\-dirs
Print the trash cans that would be scanned, home trash first, and exit.
Each rejected candidate is printed as
\fBparent_not_sticky: \fI<path>\fR
or
\fBparent_is_symlink: \fI<path>\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-trash\-dir \fIDIR\fR
List only the given trash can; the option is repeatable, the cans are
scanned in the order given.
The value replaces discovery completely and is used as\-is: no trust checks
and no
\fB\(ti\fR
expansion; a nonexistent directory is silently empty.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-users
List every account's trash cans;
\fB\-\-trash\-dir\fR
is ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-size
Print the size of the backup copy instead of the date.
.TP
.B \-\-files
Append
\fB\-> \fI<backup copy path>\fR
to each line.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-volumes
Dump volume discovery data and exit.
For debugging.
.TP
.B \-\-python
Print the path of the program's own executable and exit.
For debugging.
.PP
The last four options exist although they are undocumented upstream; they
are mainly for testing and debugging.
.SH TRASH DIRECTORIES
Unless
\fB\-\-trash\-dir\fR
is given, the cans scanned are the home trash, that is
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR
when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and non\-empty and
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR
otherwise, followed by, for every volume
\fI$volume\fR,
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash/\fI$uid\fR
and
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash\-\fI$uid\fR.
.PP
The volume list comes from
.B TRASH_VOLUMES
when set and non\-empty (colon\-separated, empty items dropped), otherwise
from the system mount table filtered to real filesystems.
.PP
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash/\fI$uid\fR
is scanned only if
\fB$volume\fB/.Trash\fR
exists, is a directory with the sticky bit, and is not a symlink; the sticky
bit is checked before the symlink.
A skipped directory is reported on standard error as
\fBTrashDir skipped because parent not sticky: \fI<path>\fR
or
\fBTrashDir skipped because parent is symlink: \fI<path>\fR.
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash\-\fI$uid\fR
is scanned whenever it is a directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including runs that printed parse warnings: an unparsable
trashinfo file, one that is not valid UTF\-8 included, is reported on
standard error and skipped.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal error, for example an unreadable
.I info/
directory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Location of the home trash,
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR.
Used only when set and non\-empty; an empty value is treated as unset, per
the XDG basedir specification, so the home trash falls back to
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR.
A relative value is used verbatim, resolved against the current directory.
.TP
.B HOME
Fallback location of the home trash,
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR.
.TP
.B TRASH_VOLUMES
Colon\-separated volume list overriding the mount table; empty items are
dropped.
.TP
.B TRASH_RAW_OUTPUT
When set, never shell\-quote paths, not even on a terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash
The home trash, when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and non\-empty.
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/share/Trash
The home trash otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.PP
.nf
trash\-list
trash\-list \-\-trash\-dirs
trash\-list \-\-trash\-dir /mnt/usb/.Trash\-1000
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trash\-put (1),
.BR trash (1),
.BR trash\-restore (1),
.BR trash\-empty (1),
.BR trash\-rm (1).
.PP
Implements the FreeDesktop.org Trash Specification,
https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash\-spec/
